          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %em Quantum electrodynamics
            (“QED”) is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            coupled to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+field"} Dirac field
            via the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron-photon+interaction"} electron-photon interaction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+theory"} quantum theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/photons"} photons
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electrons"} electrons
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+density"} Lagrangian density
            defining QED is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sum"} sum
            of
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field"} free field
                contribution of the
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field"} free field
                contribution of a
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+field"} Dirac field
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron-photon+interaction"} electron-photon interaction
                term with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} coupling constant
                given by the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fine+structure+constant"} fine structure constant
          %p
            See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A+first+idea+of+quantum+field+theory"} A first idea of quantum field theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "A+first+idea+of+quantum+field+theory#LagrangianQED"} this example
          %p
            The underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field theory
            admits a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            via the corresponding
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebra"} Wick algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            obtained from this by finding a perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            for the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron-photon+interaction"} electron-photon interaction
          %p
            As a result,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron-photon+interactions"} electron-photon interactions
            in QED are then expressed in terms of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitudes"} Feynman amplitudes
          %div
            .num_example
              %h6#example Example
              %p
                %strong
                  (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitudes"} Feynman amplitudes
                  in
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
                  – example of
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QED"} QED
              %p
                In
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
                are labeled
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multigraphs"} multigraphs
                that encode
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+of+distributions"} products of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagators"} Feynman propagators
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitudes"} Feynman amplitudes
                (
                %a{:href => "S-matrix#FeynmanPerturbationSeriesAwayFromCoincidingPoints"}> this prop.
                ) which in turn contribute to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+amplitudes"} probability amplitudes
                for physical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering"} scattering
                processes –
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitudes"} Feynman amplitudes
                are the summands in the
                = succeed "-expansion" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+matrix"} scattering matrix
              .maruku-equation
                %math#mathml_0c02e0a3528c2f6e48e15da828bd1c4874be6006_1.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒮
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %msub
                          %mi S
                          %mi int
                        %mo )
                      %mo =
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi k
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi ℕ
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %mi k
                          %mo !
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi i
                          %mi ℏ
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi k
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %munder
                        %munder
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi S
                              %mi int
                            %mo ,
                            %mi ⋯
                            %mo ,
                            %msub
                              %mi S
                              %mi int
                          %mo ⏟
                        %mrow
                          %mi k
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mtext factors
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\mathcal{S}
                      \\left(
                      S_{int}
                      \\right)
                      \=
                      \\underset{k \in \mathbb{N}}{\sum}
                      \\frac{1}{k!}
                      \\frac{1}{(i \hbar)^k}
                      T( \underset{k \, \text{factors}}{\underbrace{S_{int}, \cdots , S_{int}}} )
              %p
                of a given
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+density"} Lagrangian density
                = succeed "." do
                  %math#mathml_0c02e0a3528c2f6e48e15da828bd1c4874be6006_2.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi L
                          %mi int
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{int}
              %p
                The Feynman amplitudes are the summands in an expansion of the
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+products"} time-ordered products
                %math#mathml_0c02e0a3528c2f6e48e15da828bd1c4874be6006_3.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(\cdots)
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
                with itself, which, away from coincident vertices, is given by the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"} star product
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagator"} Feynman propagator
                %math#mathml_0c02e0a3528c2f6e48e15da828bd1c4874be6006_4.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_F
                (
                %a{:href => "S-matrix#TimeOrderedProductAwayFromDiagonal"}> this prop.
                ), via the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential"} exponential
                contraction
              .maruku-equation
                %math#mathml_0c02e0a3528c2f6e48e15da828bd1c4874be6006_5.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi int
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi int
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo =
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi prod
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi exp
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mi ℏ
                        %mo ∫
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Δ
                          %mi F
                          %mrow
                            %mi a
                            %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mfrac
                          %mi δ
                          %mrow
                            %mi δ
                            %msup
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                %mi Φ
                              %mi a
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mfrac
                          %mi δ
                          %mrow
                            %mi δ
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi Φ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi int
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi int
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      T(S_{int}, S_{int})
                      \\;=\;
                      prod \circ \exp
                      \\left(
                      \\hbar
                      \\int \Delta_{F}^{a b}(x,y)
                      \\frac{\delta}{\delta \mathbf{\Phi}^a(x)}
                      \\otimes
                      \\frac{\delta}{\delta \mathbf{\Phi}(y)}
                      \\right)
                      ( S_{int} \otimes S_{int} )
                      \\,.
              %p
                Each
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/edge"} edge
                in a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagram"} Feynman diagram
                corresponds to a factor of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagator"} Feynman propagator
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math#mathml_0c02e0a3528c2f6e48e15da828bd1c4874be6006_6.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %munder
                          %munder
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi S
                                %mi int
                              %mi ⋯
                              %msub
                                %mi S
                                %mi int
                            %mo ⏟
                          %mrow
                            %mi k
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mtext factors
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T( \underset{k \, \text{factors}}{\underbrace{S_{int} \cdots S_{int}}} )
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distribution+of+two+variables"} distribution of two variables
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex"} vertex
                corresponds to a factor of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+density"} Lagrangian density
                at
                = succeed "." do
                  %math#mathml_0c02e0a3528c2f6e48e15da828bd1c4874be6006_7.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i
              %p
                For example
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+electrodynamics"} quantum electrodynamics
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gaussian-averaged+Lorenz+gauge"} Gaussian-averaged Lorenz gauge
                involves (via
                = succeed "):" do
                  %a{:href => "S-matrix#FieldSpeciesQED"} this example
              %ol
                %li
                  %p
                    the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+field"} Dirac field
                    modelling the
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron"} electron
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagator"} Feynman propagator
                    called the
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron+propagator"} electron propagator
                    (
                    %a{:href => "Feynman+propagator#FeynmanPropagatorForDiracOperatorOnMinkowskiSpacetime"}> this def.
                    ), here to be denoted
                  .maruku-equation
                    %math#mathml_0c02e0a3528c2f6e48e15da828bd1c4874be6006_8.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Δ
                          %mphantom
                            %mi AAAA
                          %mtext electron propagator
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                          \\Delta \phantom{AAAA} \text{electron propagator}
                %li
                  %p
                    the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
                    modelling the
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/photon"} photon
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagator"} Feynman propagator
                    called the
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/photon+propagator"} photon propagator
                    (
                    %a{:href => "A+first+idea+of+quantum+field+theory#PhotonPropagatorInGaussianAveragedLorenzGauge"}> this prop.
                    ), here to be denoted
                  .maruku-equation
                    %math#mathml_0c02e0a3528c2f6e48e15da828bd1c4874be6006_9.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi G
                          %mphantom
                            %mi AAAA
                          %mtext photon propagator
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                          G \phantom{AAAA} \text{photon propagator}
                %li
                  %p
                    the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron-photon+interaction"} electron-photon interaction
                  .maruku-equation
                    %math#mathml_0c02e0a3528c2f6e48e15da828bd1c4874be6006_10.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi L
                            %mi int
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mo =
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %munder
                            %munder
                              %mrow
                                %mi i
                                %mi g
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %msup
                                  %mi γ
                                  %mi μ
                                %msup
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                  %mi α
                                %msub
                                  %mrow
                                  %mi β
                              %mo ⏟
                            %mtext interaction
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %munder
                            %munder
                              %mover
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi ψ
                                    %mi α
                                %mo ¯
                              %mo ⏟
                            %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                                  %mrow
                                    %mtext incoming
                                  %mrow
                                    %mtext electron
                              %mrow
                                %mtext field
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %munder
                            %munder
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi a
                                  %mi μ
                              %mo ⏟
                            %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                                  %mrow
                                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                                  %mrow
                                    %mtext photon
                              %mrow
                                %mtext field
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %munder
                            %munder
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mi ψ
                                  %mi β
                              %mo ⏟
                            %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                                  %mrow
                                    %mtext outgoing
                                  %mrow
                                    %mtext electron
                              %mrow
                                %mtext field
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                          L_{int}
                          \\;=\;
                          \\underset{
                          \\text{interaction}
                          }{
                          \\underbrace{
                          i g
                          (\gamma^\mu)^\alpha{}_\beta
                          }
                          }
                          \\,
                          \\underset{
                          { \text{incoming} \atop \text{electron}  }
                          \\atop
                          \\text{field}
                          }{\underbrace{\overline{\psi_\alpha}}}
                          \\;
                          \\underset{
                          {
                          \\,
                          \\atop
                          \\text{photon}
                          }
                          \\atop
                          \\text{field}
                          }{\underbrace{a_\mu}}
                          \\;
                          \\underset{
                          {\text{outgoing} \atop \text{electron} }
                          \\atop
                          \\text{field}
                          }{\underbrace{\psi^\beta}}
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagram"} Feynman diagram
                for the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron-photon+interaction"} electron-photon interaction
                alone is
              %center
                %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/InteractionVertexOfQED.jpg", :width => "150"}/
              %p
                where the solid lines correspond to the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron"} electron
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/photon"} photon
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+of+distributions"} product of distributions
                is (written in
                = succeed "-notation)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+function"} generalized function
              .maruku-equation
                %math#mathml_0c02e0a3528c2f6e48e15da828bd1c4874be6006_11.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %munder
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi ℏ
                              %mrow
                                %mn 3
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                                %mn 2
                                %mo −
                                %mn 1
                          %mo ⏟
                        %mtext loop order
                      %munder
                        %munder
                          %mrow
                            %mi i
                            %mi g
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msup
                              %mi γ
                              %mi μ
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mi α
                            %msub
                              %mrow
                              %mi β
                          %mo ⏟
                        %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                          %mrow
                            %mtext electron-photon
                          %mrow
                            %mtext interaction
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %munder
                        %munder
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mover
                                %mrow
                                  %mi Δ
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                  %mo ,
                                  %mi x
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo ¯
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                %mo ,
                                %mi α
                          %mo ⏟
                        %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                          %mrow
                            %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mtext incoming
                              %mrow
                                %mtext electron
                          %mrow
                            %mtext propagator
                      %munder
                        %munder
                          %mrow
                            %mi G
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ,
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %msub
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mrow
                                %mi μ
                                %mo ,
                                %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo ⏟
                        %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                          %mrow
                            %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                              %mrow
                                %mtext photon
                          %mrow
                            %mtext propagator
                      %munder
                        %munder
                          %mrow
                            %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ,
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mrow
                                %mi β
                                %mo ,
                                %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo ⏟
                        %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                          %mrow
                            %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mtext outgoing
                              %mrow
                                %mtext electron
                          %mrow
                            %mtext propagator
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\underset{
                      \\text{loop order}
                      }{
                      \\underbrace{
                      \\hbar^{3/2-1}
                      }
                      }
                      \\underset{
                      \\text{electron-photon}
                      \\atop
                      \\text{interaction}
                      }{
                      \\underbrace{
                      i g (\gamma^\mu)^\alpha{}_\beta
                      }
                      }
                      \\,.
                      \\,
                      \\underset{
                      {\text{incoming} \atop \text{electron}}
                      \\atop
                      \\text{propagator}
                      }{
                      \\underbrace{
                      \\overline{\Delta(-,x)}_{-, \alpha}
                      }
                      }
                      \\underset{
                      {
                      \\,
                      \\atop
                      \\text{photon}
                      }
                      \\atop
                      \\text{propagator}
                      }{
                      \\underbrace{
                      G(x,-)_{\mu,-}
                      }
                      }
                      \\underset{
                      { \text{outgoing} \atop \text{electron} }
                      \\atop
                      \\text{propagator}
                      }{
                      \\underbrace{
                      \\Delta(x,-)^{\beta, -}
                      }
                      }
              %p
                Hence a typical Feynman diagram in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QED"} QED
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
                induced by this
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron-photon+interaction"} electron-photon interaction
                looks as follows:
              %center
                %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/FeynmanDiagramGlobal.jpg", :width => "560"}/
              %p
                where on the bottom the corresponding
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitude"} Feynman amplitude
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+of+distributions"} product of distributions
                is shown; now notationally suppressing the contraction of the internal indices and all prefactors.
              %p
                For instance the two solid
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/edges"} edges
                between the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertices"} vertices
                %math#mathml_0c02e0a3528c2f6e48e15da828bd1c4874be6006_12.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_2
                and
                %math#mathml_0c02e0a3528c2f6e48e15da828bd1c4874be6006_13.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_3
                correspond to the two factors of
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math#mathml_0c02e0a3528c2f6e48e15da828bd1c4874be6006_14.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mn 2
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta(x_2,x_2)
              %center
                %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/FeynmanDiagramComponent1.jpg", :width => "560"}/
              %p
                This way each sub-graph encodes its corresponding subset of factors in the
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitude"} Feynman amplitude
              %center
                %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/FeynmanDiagramComponentTwo.jpg", :width => "560"}/
              %center
                %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/FeynmanDiagramComponentThree.jpg", :width => "560"}/
              %blockquote
                %p
                  graphics grabbed from
                  %a{:href => "Feynman+diagram#Brouder10"} Brouder 10
              %p
                A priori this
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+of+distributions"} product of distributions
                is defined away from coincident vertices:
                = succeed "." do
                  %math#mathml_0c02e0a3528c2f6e48e15da828bd1c4874be6006_15.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi i
                        %mo ≠
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi j
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i \neq x_j
                %math#mathml_0c02e0a3528c2f6e48e15da828bd1c4874be6006_16.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi j
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i = x_j
                requires a choice of
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+distributions"} extension of distributions
                to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal"} diagonal
                locus. This choice is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} ("re-")normalization
                of the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitude"} Feynman amplitude
          %h2#properties Properties
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anomalous+magnetic+moment+of+the+electron"} anomalous magnetic moment of the electron
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lamb+shift"} Lamb shift
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Casimir+effect"} Casimir effect
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axial+anomaly"} axial anomaly
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fine+structure+constant"} fine structure constant
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/photon+propagator"} photon propagator
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwinger+effect"} Schwinger effect
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field"} scalar field
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        %strong QED
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"}> QCD
                        ,
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+of+Yang-Mills+theory"} quantization of Yang-Mills theory
          %h2#references References
          %h3#original_articles Original articles
          %p The term “quantum electrodynamics” is due to
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Dirac"}> Paul Dirac
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em The quantum theory of emission and absorption of radiation
              %strong 114
              (1927) 243 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/42586?ln=en"}> spire
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://wwwhome.lorentz.leidenuniv.nl/~boyarsky/media/Proc.R.Soc.Lond.-1927-Dirac-243-65.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            This left some issues with the quantization of the radiation field. These were addressed, and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+propagator"} causal propagator
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            was first found in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pascual+Jordan"}> Pascual Jordan
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wolfgang+Pauli"} Wolfgang Pauli
              = succeed "," do
                %em Zur Quantenelektrodynamik ladungsfreier Felder
          %p
            A comprehensive, albeit informal, theory of QED and of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            in general was eventually developed by
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwinger-Tomonaga-Feynman-Dyson"}> Schwinger-Tomonaga-Feynman-Dyson
              , 1940s
          %p More on the history:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rudolf+Haag"}> Rudolf Haag
                ,
                %em Early papers on quantum field theory (1992-1930)
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/bfm%3A978-3-642-70078-1%2F1%2F1.pdf"}> pdf
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#Scharf95"} Scharf 95, section 0.0
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Silvan+S.+Schweber"}> Silvan S. Schweber
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em QED and the men who made it: Dyson, Feynman Schwinger and Tomonaga
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://press.princeton.edu/books/paperback/9780691033273/qed-and-the-men-who-made-it"} ISBN:9780691033273
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctv10crg18"} jstor:j.ctv10crg18
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://api.pageplace.de/preview/DT0400.9780691213286_A39602877/preview-9780691213286_A39602877.pdf"} pdf
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (cf.
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwinger-Tomonaga-Feynman-Dyson"} Schwinger-Tomonaga-Feynman-Dyson
          %p
            On
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+electrodynamics"} quantum electrodynamics
            via
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discretized+light-cone+quantization"} discretized light-cone quantization
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+C.+Tang"}> Andrew C. Tang
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Discretized light cone quantization: Application to quantum electrodynamics
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/literature/296776"} spire:296776
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/files/5ef318e56c87e7561d36559f54895bd1"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Tang-DLCQ.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li#TangBrodskyPauli91
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+C.+Tang"}> Andrew C. Tang
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stanley+J.+Brodsky"} Stanley J. Brodsky
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hans-Christian+Pauli"} Hans-Christian Pauli
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Discretized light-cone quantization: Formalism for quantum electrodynamics
                %strong 44
                (1991) 1842 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.44.1842"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevD.44.1842
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            Discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+stability"} vacuum stability
            in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QED"} QED
          %ul
            %li#Scharf96
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%C3%BCnter+Scharf"}> Günter Scharf
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Vacuum stability in quantum field theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/432208/"} spire:432208
            %li#Nikolic01
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hrvoje+Nikoli%C4%87"}> Hrvoje Nikolić
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Physical stability of the QED vacuum
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/0105176"} arXiv:hep-ph/0105176
          %p
            The rigorous construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative
            QED in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            is worked out in
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#Scharf95"} Scharf 95
          %p
            and refined to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+AQFT"} perturbative AQFT
            in
          %ul
            %li#DuetschFredenhagen98
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"}> Michael Dütsch
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
              %em A local (perturbative) construction of observables in gauge theores: the example of QED
              , Commun. Math. Phys. 203 (1999), no.1, 71-105, (
              %a{:href => "http://xxx.uni-augsburg.de/abs/hep-th/9807078"}> arXiv:hep-th/9807078
              ).
          %p
            and generalized to possibly non-abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            in
          %ul
            %li#Hollands07
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Hollands"}> Stefan Hollands
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Renormalized Quantum Yang-Mills Fields in Curved Spacetime
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0705.3340"} arXiv:0705.3340
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+adiabatic+limit"} weak adiabatic limit
            of QED was established in
          %ul
            %li#BlanchardSeneor75
              %p
                P. Blanchard and R. Seneor,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Green’s functions for theories with massless particles (in perturbation theory)
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item?id=AIHPA_1975__23_2_147_0"} Numdam
            %li#Duch17
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pawe%C5%82+Duch"}> Paweł Duch
                ,
                %em Massless fields and adiabatic limit in quantum field theory
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.09907"}> arXiv:1709.09907
                )
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net+of+algebras+of+observables"} local net of algebras of observables
            and hence the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+adiabatic+limit"} algebraic adiabatic limit
            was worked out in
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#DuetschFredenhagen98"} Dütsch-Fredenhagen 98
          %p
            Further discussion of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic+limit"} adiabatic limit
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infrared+divergences"} infrared divergences
            includes
          %ul
            %li
              Daniel Kapec, Malcolm Perry, Ana-Maria Raclariu,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+Strominger"} Andrew Strominger
              = succeed "," do
                %em Infrared Divergences in QED, Revisited
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.04311"} arXiv:1705.04311
          %p
            On the
            = succeed "," do
              %em field strength formalism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            of electrodynamics in terms of non-local expressions in the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strengths"} field strengths
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+densities"} flux densities
            instead of in the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+potentials"} gauge potentials
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Joshua Newey, John Terning, Christopher B. Verhaaren:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Schwinger vs Coleman: Magnetic Charge Renormalization
                %strong 2024
                75 (2024) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.13823"}> arXiv:2407.13823
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/JHEP11(2024)075"} doi:10.1007/JHEP11(2024)075
            %li
              %p
                Joshua Newey, John Terning, Christopher B. Verhaaren:
                %em Geometrizing the Anomaly
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.16998"}> arXiv:2504.16998
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %h3#review Review
          %p Traditional discussion includes
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Radovan+Dermisek"}> Radovan Dermisek
                ,
                %em Quantum Electrodynamics (QED)
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.physics.indiana.edu/~dermisek/QFT_09/qft-II-5-4p.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/DermisekQED.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hitoshi+Murayama"}> Hitoshi Murayama
                ,
                %em Quantum Electrodynamics
                (
                %a{:href => "http://hitoshi.berkeley.edu/221B-S02/QED.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#Zeidler
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eberhard+Zeidler"}> Eberhard Zeidler
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum field theory II: Quantum electrodynamics – A bridge between mathematicians and physicists
            %li
              %p
                Einan Gardi, lectures 19, 20 of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "https://www2.ph.ed.ac.uk/~egardi/MQFT/"} Modern Quantum Field Theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www2.ph.ed.ac.uk/~egardi/MQFT/MQFT_2015_lecture_19_20.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Mathematically rigorous discussion in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+AQFT"} perturbative AQFT
            is in
          %ul
            %li#Scharf95
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%C3%BCnter+Scharf"}> Günter Scharf
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Finite+Quantum+Electrodynamics+--+The+Causal+Approach"} Finite Quantum Electrodynamics – The Causal Approach
            %li#Duetsch18
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"}> Michael Dütsch
                , chapter 5 of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/From+classical+field+theory+to+perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} From classical field theory to perturbative quantum field theory
              %p
                following (
                %a{:href => "#DuetschFredenhagen98"}> Dütsch-Fredenhagen 98
                )
          %h3#phenomenology Phenomenology
          %p
            Comparison to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            is reviewed in
          %ul
            %li
              %em Tests of QED
              (
              %a{:href => "http://edu.itp.phys.ethz.ch/hs10/ppp1/PPP1_6.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            Specifically via the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lamb+shift"} Lamb shift
          %ul
            %li
              Savely G. Karshenboim, D. I. Mendeleev,
              %em The Lamb Shift of Hydrogen and Low-Energy Tests of QED
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/9411356"}> arXiv:hep-ph/9411356
              )
        .revisedby
